## The problem

The core is the part of skoro that every later entry builds on: the quantities a model is made of,
the operations that relate them, the derivatives of those operations, the log-density a model
defines, and the changes a model goes through as it is built, branched and retired. It ships no
distribution, but it must be exactly right about what a distribution, an estimator or a sampler
will ask of it, because each of those will trust it without checking.

This document works out that mathematics, at the level of precision the implementation needs.
Most of it is standard, and the standard parts are in the math notes it depends on:
[retractions](../../math/retraction/), [automatic differentiation over frames](../../math/automatic-differentiation/),
[derivatives in tangent charts](../../math/derivatives-in-charts/) and
[product manifolds](../../math/product-manifold/). What is particular to skoro, and what this
document settles, is how those pieces meet [the charter](../charter/writeup.md): which quantities
are differentiated and when, what order of derivative a computation needs from each step, what
selection and discrete quantities do to derivatives, what a query does to a model's density, how
random keys make draws reproducible, and what retiring a quantity means.

## Setting

A *model* is a set of *quantities* and a set of *terms*. Each quantity takes values in its *type*:
a manifold $\manifold$ of dimension $\dimension$ with a retraction, or a countable set. Each term
$t$ is a real-valued function $\logterm_t$ of some of the quantities, its *log-density*, and the
model's log-density is their sum (the measure theory underneath is in
[the appendix](#appendix-the-probability-space-of-a-model)),

$$
	\log \density(\point) = \sum_t \logterm_t(\point) . @tag(log-density)
$$

For a manifold-valued quantity, $\point \oplus \tangent$ and $\otherPoint \ominus \point$ are its
retraction and inverse retraction [](eq:math/retraction/oplus), and derivatives are read in tangent
charts [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/derivative). A quantity's *dimension* is that of its tangent
space; its *storage size* is the number of reals that represent a point, which may be larger (a
rotation has dimension $3$ and is stored as $9$ or $4$ numbers). Every flat vector of derivative
coordinates in this document is indexed by dimension, never by storage.

Computation is batched: a model is evaluated at $N$ points at once, one per row of a batch. Rows
never interact: an evaluation at $N$ points is $N$ independent evaluations, and every derivative
below is per row. Batching is therefore absent from the mathematics, and only the implementation
needs to keep it straight.

A model is two graphs over the same objects, and the rest of this document uses both. Each answers
different questions, and neither is ever called simply "the graph". The running example is a
vehicle whose positions are the quantities $\randomPoint_0$ and then $\randomPoint_1$, which measures
a landmark $\bfm$ once from each position, as $\bfz_0$ and $\bfz_1$. Its second position is drawn
from $\rmP_{\randomPoint_1 \mid \randomPoint_0, \bfzeta_1}$, the motion model of an inertial
measurement unit (IMU), driven by the unit's reading $\bfzeta_1$ between the two positions. The
reading is a parameter that nothing scores: it has no distribution and no prior, and a query for the
positions observes it. The IMU's model knows nothing of the platform's own physics, how far it can
plausibly move in one step, say, and that knowledge enters as a likelihood factor
$L(\randomPoint_0, \randomPoint_1, \bfsigma)$: a second motion model, over positions the first one
already scores, whose scale $\bfsigma$ is a parameter with an improper prior, the score
$S(\bfsigma)$. The example thus has a parameter with a score and one without, and shows a
likelihood factor adding information about quantities already in the model, as any number of
further factors could.

### The probabilistic graph

The *probabilistic graph* records what depends on what in distribution. Its nodes are the model's
quantities and its terms. It has an edge from each quantity a term is *conditioned on* to the term,
and an edge from each term to the quantity it *scores*, if any.

- It is bipartite: every edge joins a quantity and a term.
- Each quantity is scored by at most one term: the term of the distribution it was drawn from. A
  quantity scored by no term is a *parameter*: it has no seed distribution, and an improper prior on
  it is a score, a likelihood factor conditioned on it
  ([parameters](../../math/generative-models/#parameters-and-improper-priors)). A term that scores
  nothing is a *likelihood factor* over the quantities it is conditioned on: in Bayesian terms, the
  likelihood of data that is not a quantity of the model, such as the measurements a retirement
  conditioned on. A term that scores an observed quantity is a likelihood too, but only in the
  queries that observe it; a likelihood factor is one in every query.
- It is acyclic, because a term is conditioned only on quantities that existed before the quantity
  it scores. A topological order of it is therefore an *ancestral* order, in which each quantity
  comes after everything its distribution is conditioned on.
- Its edges are read off the compilation graph below rather than declared: a term is conditioned on the
  quantities among the roots its log-density is reached from, other than the one it scores.

<ModelGraph spec={probabilistic} />

### The compilation graph

The *compilation graph* records how values are computed. Its nodes are the model's values: its
quantities, its constants, and its *mapped values*, the outputs of operations, among which are the
terms' log-densities. It has an edge from each input of an operation to the operation's output, so
a node with incoming edges is a mapped value, computed from the nodes those edges come from.

- It is acyclic, because a model is built forward: an operation is added only after its inputs.
- Its *roots*, the nodes with no incoming edge and so no ancestor, are the quantities and the
  constants. They are the inputs of the map that the model computes, and every mapped value is a
  function of the roots it is reached from.
- A quantity is a root even when it was drawn from a distribution whose parameters are mapped
  values. That dependence belongs to the probabilistic graph above, not to this one, which is why
  no derivative ever flows through a sampler.

<ModelGraph spec={compilation} />

Evaluating a model compiles a program from the compilation graph: the subgraph of the nodes from
which a requested target is reached, its operations taken in a topological order. That subgraph is
the program's [computation graph](../../math/automatic-differentiation/#programs-and-their-computation-graphs),
and its roots are the roots of the compilation graph that the targets reach.

## Quantities

**Euclidean quantities.** The core's flat types are the real line $\R$,
vectors $\R^n$ and matrices $\R^{m \times n}$, with $\point \oplus \tangent = \point + \tangent$ and
$\otherPoint \ominus \point = \otherPoint - \point$. A matrix is flattened column by column into
$\R^{mn}$ for its tangent coordinates, so its dimension equals its storage size. The dimensions
$n$, $m$ are fixed for each quantity when it is created, and every operation checks them then.

**Positive quantities.** Scales, rates and variances live on the positive orthant
$\positive^n$, where $\positive = (0, \infty)$, with the retraction of [the example in retractions](../../math/retraction/#the-definition),
elementwise:

$$
	\point \oplus \tangent = \point \odot e^{\tangent}, \qquad
	\otherPoint \ominus \point = \log \otherPoint - \log \point . @tag(positive)
$$

A tangent coordinate is a relative change, and a point is stored as its $n$ values, so its
dimension equals its storage size. The positive orthant is not flat in the sense of the notes, since
$\oplus$ is not addition, but it is flat in disguise: $\log(\point \oplus \tangent) = \log \point +
\tangent$, so the logarithm is one chart of the whole space in which every retraction is a
translation. Three things follow. The retraction is defined for every $\tangent \in \R^n$ and never
leaves the space. The identities $(\point \oplus \tangent) \ominus \point = \tangent$ and
$\point \ominus (\point \oplus \tangent) = -\tangent$ both hold exactly. And the chart Hessian equals
the Hessian of the pull-back everywhere, not only at stationary points: in
[%full](statement:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian), $\varphi_t(u) = t\tangent + u$, whose
derivative does not move, so the correction vanishes. What is not trivial is reading a positive
quantity as a real number, through the inclusion $\positive^n \subset \R^n$: its derivative in
charts is $\operatorname{diag}(\point)$, which moves with the point.

**Embedded types.** The curved types that later entries add, a sphere for one, are most simply
written as submanifolds $\manifold \subset \R^n$ of a coordinate space, each point stored as the
vector of $\R^n$ it is. The charter has each such type added by a crate of its own, built only on
the core's public interface (position 12), and a type that needs no kernel of its own is the one
easiest to keep working as burn changes (position 11).

To every algorithm, a type is its retraction and its inverse. They make its tangent charts, and so
its derivatives, its gradients and Hessians, and the steps an estimator takes. A later type must
therefore choose them as its own geometry suggests, whatever form they take, and the core cannot
fix one form for all types. Both are arithmetic on storage: the retraction maps a stored point and a
tangent vector to a stored point, $R : \R^n \times \R^\dimension \to \R^n$, and its inverse maps two
stored points to a tangent vector, $Q : \R^n \times \R^n \to \R^\dimension$. Each is an operation
on Euclidean values, which the type writes as a composite, or as a fused operation where the
primitives cannot compose it ([Operations in feature crates](#operations-in-feature-crates)). What
the type cannot write so is the boundary between itself and its storage: the inclusion $\iota$,
which reads a point as the vector that stores it, and the retraction as an operation whose output
is a point of the type rather than a vector. Each changes type, so neither is an operation on
Euclidean values, and the derivatives of each are read in the type's charts. The core therefore
provides them once, for every *embedded type*: a type declared by its storage size $n$, its
dimension $\dimension$, its retraction $R$ and its inverse $Q$, and nothing else.

<Statement {...embedded} />

<Proof>

Fix $\point \in \manifold$. The map $\tangent \mapsto R(\point, \tangent)$ takes a neighbourhood of
$0$ into $\manifold$ and $0$ to $\point$, so its derivative $B(\point)$ at $0$ maps $\R^\dimension$
into $T_\point \manifold$, and onto it, since its rank is $\dimension$. In the coordinates that the
basis $B(\point)$ gives $T_\point \manifold$, which depend smoothly on $\point$ because $R$ is
smooth, that derivative is the identity, so $R$ is a [retraction](../../math/retraction/). By the
inverse function theorem, $R(\point, \cdot)$ is a diffeomorphism from a neighbourhood of $0$ onto a
neighbourhood of $\point$ in $\manifold$, and $Q(\point, \cdot)$ is a left inverse of it there,
hence its inverse: $\otherPoint \ominus \point = Q(\point, \otherPoint)$.

The derivatives follow from [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/derivative). For the inclusion,
$\iota(\point \oplus t u) - \iota(\point) = R(\point, t u) - \point$ has the derivative
$B(\point)\, u$ at $t = 0$. For the retraction, the chart at its output $\otherPoint$ reads a point
$z$ as $Q(\otherPoint, z)$, so its partial derivatives are those of
$t \mapsto Q(\otherPoint, R(R(\point, t u), \tangent))$ and
$t \mapsto Q(\otherPoint, R(\point, \tangent + t v))$ at $t = 0$, which are [](eq:retraction-maps)
by the chain rule. Both hold at every point of the domain, with $\otherPoint$ the output there.

For the second order, [Operations](#operations) defines $\hvp_j(\alpha)$ as the derivative of
$\partial_j\transpose \alpha$ along the curves $\point_t = \point \oplus t u$ and
$\tangent_t = \tangent + t v$, the output following as $\otherPoint_t = \point_t \oplus \tangent_t$.
The factors of $\partial_j$ are $B$, $\partial_j R$ and $L$, each smooth on an open set of a
coordinate space ($B(w) = \partial_2 R(w, 0)$ and $L(w) = \partial_2 Q(w, w)$ are defined for $w$
near $\manifold$), so the derivative of each along a curve depends only on the curve's velocity in
$\R^n$: $\dot \point = B(\point)\, u$ for $\point_t$, $v$ for $\tangent_t$, and
$\dot \otherPoint$ for $\otherPoint_t$, by the chain rule. The product rule then gives
[](eq:inclusion) and [](eq:retraction-second).

</Proof>

Every factor in [](eq:inclusion), [](eq:retraction-maps) and [](eq:retraction-second) is a pass
over $R$ or $Q$, at points the forward pass computes, with $g$ a
cotangent at the output of $R$:

| factor | pass |
| --- | --- |
| $B(\point)\, u$, $B(\point)\transpose g$ | tangent and cotangent passes over $R$ at $(\point, 0)$, at its second input |
| $\bigl(\D B(\point)[\dot \point]\bigr)\transpose g$ | a Hessian pass over $R$ at $(\point, 0)$, with velocity $\dot \point$ at its first input and cotangent $g$, read at its second |
| $\partial_j R$ and its transpose, $\bigl(\D \partial_j R\,[\dot \point, v]\bigr)\transpose L(\otherPoint)\transpose \alpha$ | tangent, cotangent and Hessian passes over $R$ at $(\point, \tangent)$, with velocities $(\dot \point, v)$ |
| $L(\otherPoint)\, \dot \otherPoint$, $L(\otherPoint)\transpose \alpha$ | tangent and cotangent passes over $Q$ at $(\otherPoint, \otherPoint)$, at its second input |
| $\bigl(\D L(\otherPoint)[\dot \otherPoint]\bigr)\transpose \alpha$ | a Hessian pass over $Q$ at $(\otherPoint, \otherPoint)$, with velocity $\dot \otherPoint$ at both inputs and cotangent $\alpha$, read at its second |

The last row seeds both inputs because $L(w) = \partial_2 Q(w, w)$ moves with both arguments of
$Q$. So the core's boundary operations are generic over embedded types: they assemble their
derivative maps from passes over the type's own operations, rather than from derivatives written by
hand, and the order of each is the most those passes admit under [%full](statement:order). The
inclusion has order two when $R$ admits a Hessian pass, and the retraction when $R$ and $Q$ both
do; each has order one when the same operations admit first-order passes. Since $B$ is itself a derivative of $R$, a second-order map of the inclusion or
the retraction needs the second derivatives of $R$: a type whose quantities are to have Hessians
declares a retraction of order two.

**Smooth as computed.** The passes in the table are taken at two places in particular: over $R$
at $\tangent = 0$, and over $Q$ on the diagonal $(\otherPoint, \otherPoint)$. Every evaluation of
a boundary operation's derivatives goes through them. [%full](statement:embedded) asks $R$ and $Q$
to be smooth there, and they are, but a composite must also *compute* them smoothly there, which is
a stronger requirement. A retraction from an exponential map, such as $R \operatorname{Exp}(\tangent)$
on the rotations, is smooth at $\tangent = 0$, yet its textbook formula divides $\sin \theta$ by
$\theta = \lVert \tangent \rVert$, and a composite of that formula computes $0/0$ at $\tangent = 0$,
and so a meaningless value for every derivative there; a logarithm written with $\operatorname{atan2}$
over $\theta$ does the same on the diagonal. So a type's $R$ and $Q$ must be smooth as computed at
$\tangent = 0$ and on the diagonal, to the order the type claims. A formula with a removable
singularity there switches near it, by a discretization of $\theta$ and
[selection](#selection), to a truncation of its Taylor series, keeping the terms through
$\theta^2$ for a type of order two, so that the derivatives the boundary operations take at the
singularity are exact. Where to switch, and how many terms to keep, is a matter of rounding on both
sides of the switch, and is the type's entry's to settle. The acceptance test's retraction and its
inverse are rational, with no such singularity.

The inverse retraction needs no boundary operation of its own, since its output is a tangent
vector. It is the composite

$$
	\otherPoint \ominus \point = Q\bigl(\iota(\point), \iota(\otherPoint)\bigr), @tag(embedded)
$$

and its derivatives are its parts'. A point of an embedded type that a model computes, rather than
creates as a quantity or a constant, comes from the retraction: from a base point the model has, or
a constant one, and a tangent vector computed in Euclidean terms, defined where the base point's
chart is. An operation that combines points of a type and lands back on it, such as the composition
of two rotations, is either a retraction from one of its inputs, valid in that input's chart, or a
fused operation of the type's crate, with its derivatives in charts written by hand and checked
against finite differences of [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/derivative). The core provides no
projection from storage onto a type. A type whose points are best combined in storage and projected
back would need one, a third boundary operation whose derivatives follow from $Q$ as the
retraction's do; the entry that first needs it adds it, without changing any type declared before.

A type often writes its retraction with a tangent basis $B$ and a projection $P$, smooth near
$\manifold$, onto it and the identity on it, as $R(\point, \tangent) = P(\point + B(\point)\, \tangent)$,
which meets the conditions of [%full](statement:embedded) because $\D P(\point)$ is the identity on
$T_\point \manifold$, and whose $\partial_2 R(\point, 0)$ is then that same $B(\point)$. The core
does not impose that form. The retraction of
[the acceptance test](#the-acceptance-test) is not of it, and a type whose geometry suggests another
retraction declares that one, and its derivatives follow all the same. The conditions are asked only of $\manifold$, which may be the part of a
manifold that the type's retraction covers smoothly: no basis is smooth on the whole sphere, for
one, so the acceptance test's $\manifold$ is the sphere without a point. Where a type's points may
stray outside it, and what then happens, is that type's entry's question, not the core's.

The positive orthant meets the conditions too, as an open subset of $\R^n$ with
$\dimension = n$, $R(\point, \tangent) = \point \odot e^{\tangent}$ and
$Q(\point, \otherPoint) = \log \otherPoint - \log \point$, and its $B(\point)$ is
$\operatorname{diag}(\point)$. The core keeps it as a type of its own all the same, with its
operations written in its logarithmic chart, where they are linear.

**Discrete quantities.** The core's discrete types are the finite sets
$\mathrm{Categorical}(n) = \{0, \dots, n - 1\}$, with $\mathrm{Boolean} = \mathrm{Categorical}(2)$,
and the integers $\mathbb Z$. A discrete quantity has no tangent space, no retraction and no
dimension, and it is never a coordinate of a derivative. Its values are written
$\discretePoint$. A density over a discrete quantity is a probability mass, a density with respect
to the counting measure.

**Products.** A product of continuous types is a continuous type: a quantity of type
$\manifold_1 \times \cdots \times \manifold_n$ is one point $(\point_1, \dots, \point_n)$, stored as
its factors' storage concatenated, with the stacked tangent space
[](eq:math/product-manifold/stack) and the factor-wise retraction of
[%full](statement:math/product-manifold/product-retraction). The core has products of continuous
types only. A quantity with a continuous part and a discrete part is two quantities, as the charter
has it, and a joint density over several discrete quantities needs no product, since a term may be
conditioned on any number of quantities.

A product's factors may be of any continuous type, including products, and of dimensions known
only at run time. That is what makes a product the natural type of a marginal over a Markov
blanket, whose members are only known when a quantity is retired ([Retirement](#retirement)).

## Operations

An *operation* is a smooth map

$$
	\otherPoint = \map(\point_1, \dots, \point_m; \discretePoint_1, \dots, \discretePoint_r) @tag(operation)
$$

from continuous inputs $\point_j \in \manifold_j$ and discrete inputs $\discretePoint_i$ to an
output $\otherPoint \in \otherManifold$, smooth in the continuous inputs for each value of the
discrete ones. In the compilation graph it is a mapped node with an incoming edge from each input.
Its partial derivatives $\partial_j \map$ are taken in tangent charts, and it may provide four maps,
each over a *frame* of directions
([automatic differentiation over frames](../../math/automatic-differentiation/)):

| map | given | returns |
| --- | --- | --- |
| forward | the inputs | $\otherPoint$ |
| tangent | a frame $\tangent_j$ per continuous input | $\otherTangent = \sum_j \partial_j \map\, \tangent_j$ |
| cotangent | a frame $\alpha$ at the output | $\partial_j \map\transpose \alpha$ per continuous input |
| second order | velocities $\tangent_j$, and one cotangent $\alpha$ | $\hvp_j(\alpha) = \frac{\dd}{\dd t}\big\vert_{t=0} \partial_j \map(\point \oplus t\tangent)\transpose \alpha$ per input |

The second-order map takes a velocity frame of several columns, one Hessian direction each, and a
single cotangent column. Its definition in charts is the one under which a forward-over-reverse pass
computes the chart Hessian [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian), by
[%full](statement:math/derivatives-in-charts/passes).

Discrete inputs carry no derivatives, and a discrete output carries none either. A map that turns a
discrete input into a continuous output (an *embedding*, such as an integer read as a real) has no
partial derivative in it. A map that turns a continuous input into a discrete output (a
*discretization*, such as the indicator $\mathbb 1[\point \ge 0]$) passes no cotangent back. For a
step function that is the true derivative everywhere but at its steps, and the core makes no
attempt to do better: a model that needs a derivative across a discretization needs a relaxation,
which is a modelling choice, not the core's.

### Two gates

A pass runs over a program's computation graph, whose roots include the quantities it
differentiates with respect to. Within a pass, an operation's continuous inputs differ in two ways
that the implementation must keep apart:

- an input is *differentiable* if it is reached from a root the pass differentiates with respect
  to, which decides whether cotangents ($\alpha$, and in a Hessian pass $\beta$) are pulled back
  into it along the
  [backpropagation graph](../../math/automatic-differentiation/#reverse-pulling-cotangent-frames-over-the-backpropagation-graph);
- an input *carries a velocity* if it is reached from a root seeded with a velocity in this pass,
  which decides whether its velocity enters $\hvp$.

The second implies the first, and not conversely: a Hessian pass is often seeded at one quantity
while all of them are differentiated. An operation's $\hvp_j$ is absent whenever $\partial_j \map$
is not needed, and a missing velocity counts as zero.

### Order

Every operation states its *order* $\order(\map)$: $0$ if it provides only the forward map, $1$ if
it also provides the tangent and cotangent maps, $2$ if it also provides the second-order map. Most
primitives have order $2$. An operation that runs burn's automatic differentiation inside, such as a
neural network, has order $1$; one whose derivative does not exist or was not written has order
$0$. What a pass needs of a step depends on where the step sits in the program's computation graph.

<Statement {...order} />

A pass that is not admissible is refused before it runs, naming a step $k$ with
$\order(\map_k) < \req(k)$. This refines the charter's "least order over the program's steps" in two
ways that matter in practice. A step that no differentiated root reaches, or that reaches no target,
needs nothing, so a model may contain a first-order neural network and still be differentiated
twice with respect to quantities that do not feed it. And a step that only pulls back cotangents in
a Hessian pass, because no seeded velocity reaches it, needs only first order: its $\hvp$ terms are
zero by [](eq:math/automatic-differentiation/leibniz).

## Selection

*Selection* chooses one of several continuous values by a discrete index:

$$
	\otherPoint = \operatorname{select}(\discretePoint;\, \point_0, \dots, \point_{n-1}) = \point_\discretePoint,
	\qquad \discretePoint \in \mathrm{Categorical}(n),\; \point_i \in \manifold . @tag(select)
$$

All the $\point_i$ and $\otherPoint$ are of one type. For each $\discretePoint$ the map is a
projection, so its derivatives in charts are as simple as derivatives get: since
$\otherPoint = \point_\discretePoint$, the chart at $\otherPoint$ is the chart at
$\point_\discretePoint$, and

$$
	\partial_{\point_i} \otherPoint = \begin{cases} I & i = \discretePoint, \\ 0 & i \ne \discretePoint, \end{cases}
	\qquad \hvp_{\point_i} = 0 .
$$

The cotangent at $\otherPoint$ goes to the selected value and nowhere else, and the index receives
nothing. Mixtures, switching models and data association are built on selection: a mixture whose
component is a discrete quantity reads that component's parameters through selection, and a
measurement associated by a discrete quantity selects the target it came from. The derivative with
respect to the values, at a fixed index, is exactly what a gradient-based algorithm conditioned on
the discrete quantities needs.

## The primitives

The core ships all of the primitive operations below, with the derivative maps they carry; those
on Euclidean quantities first. In
the table, $\odot$ is the elementwise product, $\tangent_a$ the velocity of an input $a$ in a
Hessian pass (or its tangent, in the tangent map), and $\alpha$ the cotangent at the output. Each
frame is handled column by column, so one column is shown.

| operation | tangent | cotangent per input | second order per input |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $a + b$ | $\tangent_a + \tangent_b$ | $\alpha,\ \alpha$ | $0,\ 0$ |
| $a - b$ | $\tangent_a - \tangent_b$ | $\alpha,\ -\alpha$ | $0,\ 0$ |
| $-a$ | $-\tangent_a$ | $-\alpha$ | $0$ |
| $a \odot b$ | $\tangent_a \odot b + a \odot \tangent_b$ | $b \odot \alpha,\ a \odot \alpha$ | $\tangent_b \odot \alpha,\ \tangent_a \odot \alpha$ |
| $s\,a$, $s \in \R$ | $\tangent_s\, a + s\, \tangent_a$ | $a\transpose \alpha,\ s\, \alpha$ | $\tangent_a\transpose \alpha,\ \tangent_s\, \alpha$ |
| $A B$ | $\tangent_A B + A \tangent_B$ | $\alpha B\transpose,\ A\transpose \alpha$ | $\alpha \tangent_B\transpose,\ \tangent_A\transpose \alpha$ |
| $\sum_i a_i$ | $\sum_i (\tangent_a)_i$ | $\alpha \mathbf 1$ | $0$ |
| $e^{a}$ (elementwise) | $e^{a} \odot \tangent_a$ | $e^{a} \odot \alpha$ | $e^{a} \odot \tangent_a \odot \alpha$ |
| $\log a$ (elementwise) | $\tangent_a / a$ | $\alpha / a$ | $-\tangent_a \odot \alpha / a^2$ |
| $\sin a$ (elementwise) | $\cos a \odot \tangent_a$ | $\cos a \odot \alpha$ | $-\sin a \odot \tangent_a \odot \alpha$ |
| $\cos a$ (elementwise) | $-\sin a \odot \tangent_a$ | $-\sin a \odot \alpha$ | $-\cos a \odot \tangent_a \odot \alpha$ |
| $\operatorname{atan2}(a, b)$ (elementwise) | $(b \odot \tangent_a - a \odot \tangent_b) / r$ | $b \odot \alpha / r,\ -a \odot \alpha / r$ | [](eq:atan2) |
| slice, concatenate, transpose | the same map | its adjoint | $0$ |
| select | [](eq:select) | to the selected value | $0$ |

The trigonometric rows are there because the retraction that a curved type's geometry suggests is
often built from sines and cosines, and its inverse from an inverse tangent: with them, such a
retraction is a composite, whose derivatives follow from the table, rather than a fused operation
whose derivatives are written by hand, with no composite to fall back on while an upgrade of burn
breaks its kernels. $\operatorname{atan2}(a, b) \in (-\pi, \pi]$ is the angle of the point
$(b, a)$, defined where $r = a \odot a + b \odot b$ is positive. Its derivatives, with the second
order read off the first by differentiating $b / r$ and $-a / r$, are

$$
	\hvp_a = \frac{(a^2 - b^2) \odot \tangent_b - 2\, a \odot b \odot \tangent_a}{r^2} \odot \alpha,
	\qquad
	\hvp_b = \frac{(a^2 - b^2) \odot \tangent_a + 2\, a \odot b \odot \tangent_b}{r^2} \odot \alpha, @tag(atan2)
$$

squares, products and quotients taken elementwise. They hold off the half-line $a = 0$, $b < 0$,
where the angle jumps by $2\pi$; there the map is a discretization in the sense of
[Operations](#operations), and a composite that must cross it, as an inverse retraction near a half
turn may, switches formulas by [selection](#selection).

The operations on positive quantities read their tangents and cotangents in the charts
[](eq:positive), that is, in logarithmic coordinates, where all but the inclusion are linear. Here
$a$ and $b$ are positive, $c$ is Euclidean, and the products, quotients and powers are
elementwise.

| operation | from, to | tangent | cotangent per input | second order per input |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $a$ as a real | $\positive^n \to \R^n$ | $a \odot \tangent_a$ | $a \odot \alpha$ | $a \odot \tangent_a \odot \alpha$ |
| $e^{c}$ | $\R^n \to \positive^n$ | $\tangent_c$ | $\alpha$ | $0$ |
| $\log a$ | $\positive^n \to \R^n$ | $\tangent_a$ | $\alpha$ | $0$ |
| $a \odot b$ | $\positive^n \times \positive^n \to \positive^n$ | $\tangent_a + \tangent_b$ | $\alpha,\ \alpha$ | $0,\ 0$ |
| $1 / a$ | $\positive^n \to \positive^n$ | $-\tangent_a$ | $-\alpha$ | $0$ |

The exponential and logarithm here are not the rows of the first table: those map $\R^n$ to
$\R^n$, read in the flat charts, where their derivatives are $e^{a}$ and $1/a$; these change type,
and in the charts of the positive side their derivatives are identities. Both are kept, because a
model may want either. The retraction [](eq:positive) is the composite $\point \odot e^{\tangent}$
of the second and fourth rows, and its inverse $\log \otherPoint - \log \point$ of the third and a
Euclidean subtraction, so every derivative of both, of either order, is an identity or zero.

Beside the rows of both tables, every embedded type has the two boundary operations of
[Embedded types](#quantities), its inclusion $\iota$ and its retraction $\oplus$. Their derivative
maps [](eq:inclusion), [](eq:retraction-maps) and [](eq:retraction-second) are assembled from
passes over the type's own operations rather than written in a table.

Every row is checked the same way: the tangent map is the derivative of the forward map, the
cotangent map its transpose, and the second-order map the derivative of the cotangent map along the
velocities, holding $\alpha$ fixed. The bilinear operations ($a \odot b$, $s\,a$, $A B$) and
$\operatorname{atan2}$ are the only ones whose second-order maps mix inputs: the pull-back into one
input involves the other, so the other's velocity appears. The linear ones have none. The discrete operations the core needs
(integer arithmetic and comparison) and the embedding of an integer as a real carry no
derivatives, by the conventions of [Operations](#operations).

## Programs, composites and passes

A model is evaluated by compiling a program from the compilation graph, as in
[The compilation graph](#the-compilation-graph). Four passes run over a compiled program: the
forward pass, the tangent pass [](eq:math/automatic-differentiation/jvp) along the edges of its
computation graph, the cotangent pass [](eq:math/automatic-differentiation/vjp) along the edges of
its backpropagation graph, and the Hessian pass [](eq:math/automatic-differentiation/leibniz), which
runs both. Derivatives are collected at the program's roots only, which are the leaves of its
backpropagation graph, and [Queries](#queries) says which roots those are.

A *composite* operation is a named subgraph of the compilation graph, such as the retraction of a
product, or (in later entries) a distribution's log-density: a set of operations with designated
inputs and one output, stored under one name. Its derivative maps are *defined* as the composition
of its parts', so expanding a composite into its parts at compilation changes no value that any
pass computes, and its order is that of its parts under [%full](statement:order). A hand-written
fused form of a composite, introduced for speed or for numerical care, is a different operation
with the same mathematics, and it is correct only if every pass over it agrees with every pass over
the composite, to within rounding. Feature crates write such operations too, as
[Operations in feature crates](#operations-in-feature-crates) says.

The product retraction is the composite the charter's acceptance test is about.

<Statement {...productAgreement} />

<Proof>

Slicing and concatenation are linear, so their tangent maps are themselves, their cotangent maps
are their adjoints (scattering into a zero block and gathering a block), and their second-order maps
are zero. In the tangent pass, slicing hands each factor's retraction exactly its blocks of the
frames, and concatenation stacks what they return. In the cotangent and Hessian passes, the
concatenation's adjoint hands each factor exactly its blocks of $\alpha$ and $\beta$, the factors
pull them back, and the slicing's adjoint places each result in its own block, adding only zeros. By
[](eq:math/automatic-differentiation/leibniz), with the linear steps' $\hvp$ zero, the product's
$\beta$ is the factors' $\beta$s, stacked. So every pass computes the block-diagonal assembly of the
factors' passes, which is the derivative structure of [](eq:math/product-manifold/blocks).

</Proof>

The proof introduces no arithmetic beyond additions of zero, so a test may hold the agreement to a
tolerance of a few units in the last place.

### Operations in feature crates

Every operation in a model is of one of three kinds, and the kind decides where it may be written.

- **Primitives** are the core's alone: the operations of [The primitives](#the-primitives), with
  derivative maps and kernels written by hand, and the boundary operations of embedded types, whose
  derivative maps are assembled from passes over each type's retraction and its inverse.
- **Composites** may be written anywhere, in a feature crate as in the core. They are built from
  other operations, their derivative maps are those of their parts by the chain rule, and they
  touch no tensor.
- **Fused operations** may be written anywhere too. A fused operation provides its forward,
  tangent, cotangent and second-order maps itself, as a primitive does, computed by kernels of its
  own over the core's public tensor layer, and it states its order like any operation.

A fused operation is what a feature crate writes when a composite is too slow, or loses precision
where the feature needs it: a bespoke kernel for the exponential of $\operatorname{SO}(3)$, say, or
for its logarithm near angle $\pi$. It owes three things.

1. **A reference.** It names the composite it fuses, when one can be written, and every pass over
   it agrees with every pass over that composite, to within rounding, at both precisions. When
   none can, its derivative maps are checked against finite differences of
   [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/derivative), at both precisions, as a primitive's are.
2. **Every backend.** It runs on every backend skoro supports, or runs the composite it fuses on
   the backends its kernels do not cover.
3. **A name.** It is registered under a name, as every operation is, so that a serialized model
   names it and a model read without the crate that defines it is refused, naming the operation,
   rather than misread.

This is what keeps the core stable as feature crates arrive. The core's primitives are the
operations every feature shares (arithmetic, exponentials and logarithms, trigonometric functions,
products of matrices, slicing and selection), and no feature's needs, however particular, add to them: what composes is
a composite, and what must be faster or more careful than its composite is a fused operation in
the feature's own crate. The core changes for a feature only when the feature needs something its
public interface cannot express, such as the boundary operations of embedded types, and such a
change is general: it is made once, for every later feature of the kind. The cost of a fused
operation falls on its own crate, whose kernels are written against the tensor layer and may need
changes when burn does. A composite bounds that cost. It is a correct operation with the same
mathematics, so while a fused operation's kernels are broken by an upgrade, its feature can use the
composite in its place, slower but right, and the upgrade does not wait on the feature. That is why
a fused operation should have a composite whenever one can be written, and why numerical care is
first sought in the composite itself, where it can often be had: a logarithm of
$\operatorname{SO}(3)$ can switch formulas near angle $\pi$ by a discretization of the angle and
[selection](#selection), whose derivatives are exact away from the switch.

The mathematics here assumes the tensor layer is public: the tensor shapes skoro uses (batched
points, tangent frames, integer values) and the operations on them that kernels need. What it
exposes, and how, is the implementation's to settle.

### The acceptance test

The charter accepts its position on products on one condition: that the retraction of a runtime
product, and its first and second derivatives, agree with the factor-wise computation. On the
core's own types that is a weak test at second order. In the charts of every one of them a
retraction is a translation, so every second-order map along it is zero, and the second
derivatives under test are zeros on both sides. The test is therefore run on a product that
includes a curved factor, and composed with a function whose second derivative does not vanish.

**A sphere outside the core.** The test's curved factor is the unit sphere
$S^2 = \{\point \in \R^3 : \lVert \point \rVert = 1\}$, of dimension $2$ and storage size $3$,
declared as an embedded type. The test is written as a later entry's crate would be written,
outside the core and with nothing but its public interface, so that it also shows a curved type
being added with no derivative written by hand. The sphere is not a type of the core. skoro means
to support it, but in an entry of its own, which settles what this test can leave aside: a basis
for the whole sphere, the choice of retraction, and the sphere's reference measure. When that entry
adds it, the acceptance test runs on that sphere instead, and this one is removed.

The tangent plane at $\point$ is $\point^\perp$. No basis of it is smooth on the whole sphere (a
smooth field of tangent bases on $S^2$ would give a nowhere-vanishing tangent vector field, which
the hairy ball theorem rules out), so the test's basis is smooth on $U = \{\point : \point_3 > -1\}$,
the sphere without its south pole, and the tests stay inside it:

$$
	B(\point) = \begin{pmatrix}
		1 - c\, \point_1^2 & -c\, \point_1 \point_2 \\
		-c\, \point_1 \point_2 & 1 - c\, \point_2^2 \\
		-\point_1 & -\point_2
	\end{pmatrix},
	\qquad c = \frac{1}{1 + \point_3} .
$$

Its columns are orthogonal to $\point$ and orthonormal on the sphere, as expanding with
$\point_1^2 + \point_2^2 = (1 - \point_3)(1 + \point_3)$ shows: the first column's product with
$\point$, for one, is $\point_1 (1 - \point_3) - c\, \point_1 (1 - \point_3^2) = 0$. The test's
retraction is the inverse of the stereographic projection from $-\point$, in coordinates scaled so
that its derivative at $0$ is $B(\point)$, and needs no projection: with
$s = \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4$,

$$
	\point \oplus \tangent = \frac{(1 - s)\, \point + B(\point)\,\tangent}{1 + s},
	\qquad
	\otherPoint \ominus \point = Q(\point, \otherPoint) = \frac{2\, B(\point)\transpose \otherPoint}{1 + \point\transpose \otherPoint}
	\quad (\point\transpose \otherPoint > -1) . @tag(sphere)
$$

The retraction stays on the sphere because $\point\transpose B(\point) = 0$ and
$B(\point)\transpose B(\point) = I$ give
$\lVert (1 - s)\, \point + B(\point)\,\tangent \rVert^2 = (1 - s)^2 + 4 s = (1 + s)^2$, and its
derivative in $\tangent$ at $0$ is $B(\point)$, since $s$ is quadratic. For the inverse, the same
identities give $\point\transpose \otherPoint = (1 - s)/(1 + s)$ and
$B(\point)\transpose \otherPoint = \tangent / (1 + s)$, so $1 + \point\transpose \otherPoint = 2/(1 + s)$
and $\tangent = 2\, B(\point)\transpose \otherPoint / (1 + \point\transpose \otherPoint)$. The
retraction is defined for every $\tangent$ and reaches every point but $-\point$, where its inverse
is undefined. It is not of the form $P(\point + B(\point)\,\tangent)$ for the normalizing
projection, which would go in the direction of $\point + B(\point)\,\tangent$ rather than of
$(1 - s)\, \point + B(\point)\,\tangent$, so the test exercises a retraction that the core knows
only as an operation. Both $R$ and $Q$ are composites of the core's Euclidean primitives. The
reciprocals are exponentials of logarithms, $c = e^{-\log(1 + \point_3)}$,
$1 / (1 + s) = e^{-\log(1 + s)}$ and
$1 / (1 + \point\transpose \otherPoint) = e^{-\log(1 + \point\transpose \otherPoint)}$, each
logarithm taken of a number positive on the domain; the rest is products, sums, slicing and
concatenation.

Unlike the core's types, the sphere is curved: its tangent charts do not commute, the correction term of
[%full](statement:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian) is not zero away from stationary
points, and the second-order maps of its boundary operations are not zero, since its basis turns
as the point moves.

**What it compares.** The test builds a runtime product of two Euclidean factors, a real line, a
positive factor and the sphere, and a nonlinear scalar function $f$ on it that reads the positive
factor and the sphere as real numbers, through their inclusions. It checks, at points of $U$:

1. that every pass over the product's retraction, values, tangent frames, cotangent frames and
   Hessian products, agrees with the factor-wise computation, block by block, as
   [%full](statement:product-agreement) says it must;
2. that the chart gradient and the chart Hessian products of $f$ through the product's retraction,
   computed by the passes, agree with the factor-wise assembly of
   [](eq:math/product-manifold/blocks), and with finite differences of their definitions
   [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/gradient) and [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian);
3. that on the sphere's block, away from a stationary point, the chart Hessian differs from the
   Hessian of the pull-back by the correction term, and on every other block it does not.

The sphere's boundary operations are checked the same way first, [](eq:inclusion),
[](eq:retraction-maps) and [](eq:retraction-second) against finite differences of
[](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/derivative), and its $Q$ against the retraction it must invert, so
that a failure of the acceptance test points at the product rather than at the type.

## Queries

The charter puts conditioning in the query: the model holds the joint density
[](eq:log-density), and each question asked of it says which quantities are fixed. A *query*
partitions the quantities its targets reach in the compilation graph twice over, into observed and
free, and into continuous and discrete:

- the *observed* quantities, continuous $\point_O$ and discrete $\discretePoint_O$, fixed at values
  the query gives: what the query conditions on;
- the *free* quantities, continuous $\point_F$ and discrete $\discretePoint_F$: the variables of the
  query, whose values are the point at which it is evaluated.

The query's log-density is then a function of the free values,

$$
	\log \density(\point_F, \discretePoint_F;\, \point_O, \discretePoint_O) = \sum_t \logterm_t(\point_F, \discretePoint_F, \point_O, \discretePoint_O), @tag(query)
$$

which is, up to an additive constant, the log-density of the conditional distribution of the free quantities given the observed ones, with respect to Lebesgue
measure on the continuous free quantities and counting measure on the discrete ones. One model
answers many queries: with the measurements observed it is a posterior; with nothing observed, a
prior to simulate from; with the states observed, a likelihood of measurements. A switching model's
mode, or the association of a measurement with the landmark it came from, is observed when it is
known and free when it is not. Parameters are quantities like any other here: a
query for the example's positions observes the IMU reading $\bfzeta_1$ along with the measurements,
and either observes the scale $\bfsigma$ or leaves it free, to be estimated with the positions.

**Roots.** The roots of a query's program, the roots of its computation graph, are the
quantities and constants its targets reach. Of these, the free continuous quantities are the ones
every pass differentiates with respect to, in their tangent charts; the observed quantities, the
free discrete ones and the constants are inputs no pass differentiates. A quantity's value is an input of the query even
when the quantity was drawn from a distribution whose parameters depend on other quantities, since
it is a root of the compilation graph: $\partial \log \density / \partial \point_i$ counts
$\point_i$'s appearances in the terms, never its role in generating its children.

**Folding.** A mapped value whose inputs are all fixed is itself fixed, and need not be computed on
every evaluation: it can be computed once and put in place of the subgraph that computes it, as a
new root. Nothing a pass computes changes, since no derivative flows into a fixed value. A value is
fixed at one of two levels, and is folded at that level.

- **In the model.** A value reached only from constants is the same in every query, snapshot and
  branch, so it is folded once, when it is added to the compilation graph, and the model keeps the
  result, in double precision on the host like every value it stores. A query casts it to its own
  precision like any other constant. Conditioning in the query does not prevent this: a constant is
  not a quantity, so no query can observe or free it.
- **In a compiled query.** A value reached from observed quantities, continuous or discrete, and
  from constants, is fixed only for the queries that observe those quantities, so it is folded when
  such a query is compiled, and reused by every evaluation of the compiled program.

A value reached from a free quantity is not folded. The difference shows in a Gaussian term written
with a covariance factor $L$, whose log-density reads its inverse and the logarithms of its diagonal
(a later entry decides how the Gaussian takes its covariance). If $L$ is a constant, both are
folded once in the model; if it is an observed quantity, once per compiled query; if it is free,
they are computed on every evaluation, together with their derivatives with respect to $L$.

Selection by an observed index folds further than its value. Since
$\operatorname{select}(\discretePoint;\, \point_0, \dots, \point_{n-1})$ is $\point_\discretePoint$,
an observed $\discretePoint$ makes the selection the chosen input itself, and the compiled program
takes that input in its place. The inputs not chosen, and every value that reaches the program's
targets only through them, drop out of the program: a mixture with its component observed computes
that component alone.

**Discrete quantities in a query.** Observing a discrete quantity and leaving it free give the same
density at the same values, since [](eq:query) is the same sum either way. What differs is the
distribution the query targets. An observed $\discretePoint_O$ is conditioned on: the target is the
distribution of the free quantities given its value, which every evaluation holds. A free
$\discretePoint_F$ is part of the target: an algorithm evaluates the density at values of
$\discretePoint_F$ it chooses (the current state of a Markov chain, or a drawn particle) and must
sum or sample over them to answer for the target. Derivatives stop at discrete quantities, so the
gradient of [](eq:query) at given values of $\discretePoint_F$ is the gradient of the conditional
density of $\point_F$ given those values, not of the target. A gradient cannot sum or sample out
$\discretePoint_F$, so a gradient-based algorithm refuses a query with a free discrete quantity
(charter, position 7), rather than silently condition on the values it was given. Observing the
discrete quantities is how a model that has them is given to such an algorithm.

**Flattened coordinates.** An estimator works with vectors, not with quantities. Ordered as the
estimator chooses, the free continuous quantities $\point_F = (\point_1, \dots, \point_n)$ are one point of the
product manifold $\manifold_1 \times \cdots \times \manifold_n$, a tangent of which is a vector
$\tangent \in \R^\dimension$, $\dimension = \sum_i \dimension_i$, stacked by
[](eq:math/product-manifold/stack). An estimator's step is that product's retraction,

$$
	\point_F \oplus \tangent = (\point_1 \oplus \tangent_1, \dots, \point_n \oplus \tangent_n), @tag(step)
$$

and a Jacobian, gradient or Hessian over $\point_F$ is the block matrix of
[](eq:math/product-manifold/blocks). So the layout an estimator uses and a product quantity type are
the same mathematical object, and the core should build them from one construction.

## Terms and the importance weight

A term $t$ is a node of the probabilistic graph. Its log-density $\logterm_t$ is a node of the
compilation graph, computed from the quantities it is reached from there. Following the charter, a
term records the quantity it *scores*, $s_t$, if any, and the quantities it is *conditioned on*,
$C_t$, so that $\logterm_t$ reads as $\log \density_t(s_t \mid C_t)$. The quantities a term
involves, $V_t = \{s_t\} \cup C_t$, its neighbours in the probabilistic graph, determine every
structural question the core answers: which terms touch a quantity, what a quantity's Markov
blanket is, and what retiring it disturbs. They are only right if they contain every quantity
$\logterm_t$ depends on, so the core reads $V_t$ off the compilation graph, as the quantities among
the roots $\logterm_t$ is reached from, rather than trusting a declaration; $C_t$ is then $V_t$
without $s_t$. In the example, $\logterm_6$ is reached from $\randomPoint_0$, $\randomPoint_1$
and $\bfsigma$ (and two constants, which are not quantities), and the term $t_6$ scores nothing,
so it is conditioned on all three: it is the likelihood factor $L(\randomPoint_0, \randomPoint_1,
\bfsigma)$. The motion term's $\logterm_3$ is reached from $\randomPoint_0$, $\randomPoint_1$ and
$\bfzeta_1$, and $t_3$ scores $\randomPoint_1$, so it is conditioned on $\randomPoint_0$ and
$\bfzeta_1$: it is the density of $\rmP_{\randomPoint_1 \mid \randomPoint_0, \bfzeta_1}$.

**The generative reading.** A distribution may provide a sampler for the quantity it scores, which
draws $s_t$ given $C_t$, with a log-density $\log \proposal_t(s_t \mid C_t)$ of its own; an exact
sampler has $\proposal_t = \density_t$ (charter, position 9).

A parameter has no distribution, and so no sampler; the core supplies a *proposal* for it instead.
A proposal is a conditional density $\proposal_\theta(\theta \mid C_\theta)$ attached to the
parameter $\theta$ in the model, with respect to the parameter's base measure and conditioned on
quantities $C_\theta$ that existed before the parameter, from which the parameter is drawn when it
is generated. It is not a term: it contributes nothing to $\log \density$, and enters only the
weights below. Ancestral order places each parameter after its $C_\theta$, as it places a scored
quantity after the quantities its term is conditioned on. A parameter without a proposal is legal,
as a distribution without a sampler is.

A query generates its free quantities, continuous and discrete, ancestrally: each scored quantity
from the sampler of the term that scores it, and each parameter from its proposal, in a
topological order of the probabilistic graph. In this section, $\point_F$ stands for all of them,
$\discretePoint_F$ included, $\point_O$ for all the observed quantities, $\discretePoint_O$
included, and $\Theta_F$ for the parameters among the free ones. Generation is possible exactly when every free
quantity is scored by a term with a sampler or is a parameter with a proposal; it is refused
otherwise, naming a quantity that is neither. The importance weight of a generated point is

$$
	\log \weight(\point_F) = \sum_t \logterm_t(\point_F, \point_O)
	- \sum_{t \,:\, s_t \in F} \log \proposal_t(s_t \mid C_t)
	- \sum_{\theta \in \Theta_F} \log \proposal_\theta(\theta \mid C_\theta), @tag(weight)
$$

which is the charter's [](eq:archive/charter/writeup/weight), with the observed quantities' terms,
the likelihood factors and the parameters' scores, the likelihoods of this query, contributing
$\logterm_t$ alone, each term whose sampler is exact cancelling, and each free parameter
contributing its proposal. An optimizer generates nothing, and needs a starting value for each free
parameter instead: the query gives one, or it is drawn from the parameter's proposal.

<Statement {...importance} />

<Proof>

Ancestral generation draws $\point_F$ with density
$\proposal(\point_F) = \prod_{t : s_t \in F} \proposal_t(s_t \mid C_t) \prod_{\theta \in \Theta_F} \proposal_\theta(\theta \mid C_\theta)$
with respect to the product of the base measures, since each free quantity is drawn given
quantities drawn or fixed before it. Then
$\weight = \density(\point_F, \point_O) / \proposal(\point_F)$ wherever $\proposal > 0$, which
covers the set where $\density(\cdot, \point_O) > 0$ by the positivity condition, and
$\E_\proposal[\weight h] = \int h\, \density(\point_F, \point_O)\, \dd \point_F$. When $Z$ is
finite and positive, $\pi = \density(\cdot, \point_O)\, \dd\point_F / Z$ is a probability
measure, and the integral is $Z\, \E_\pi[h]$.

</Proof>

Two conditions in the statement are the core's to check or to state. The base measures must match.
In the core, a continuous quantity's density is with respect to Lebesgue measure on the space its
values lie in, $\R^n$ or the open subset $\positive^n$ of it, and a discrete quantity's with
respect to counting measure, so this holds as long as every distribution keeps to that convention.
The one place it is easy to break is a density written in the logarithmic chart of a positive
quantity, such as a log-normal written as a normal in $u = \log \point$. If $g$ is the density of
$\log \randomPoint$ with respect to Lebesgue measure on $\R^n$, the density $f$ of $\randomPoint$
with respect to Lebesgue measure on $\positive^n$ carries the Jacobian of the chart,

$$
	\log f(\point) = \log g(\log \point) - \sum_i \log \point_i . @tag(log-chart-density)
$$

Densities on manifolds without such a chart will need a base measure of their own when the first
of them arrives. Positivity is the distribution's responsibility, and the core cannot check it.

## Random keys

Every draw comes from a key (charter, position 10). Mathematically a key is an element of a finite
set $\mathcal K$, with two deterministic maps: $\operatorname{split} : \mathcal K \to \mathcal K^n$,
and $\operatorname{fold} : \mathcal K \times \Sigma \to \mathcal K$, which folds in one address
segment from the set $\Sigma$ of names and integers. The key of the quantity at address
$a = a_1 / \cdots / a_m$ in a run with key $\key$ is

$$
	\key_a = \operatorname{fold}(\cdots \operatorname{fold}(\operatorname{fold}(\key, a_1), a_2) \cdots, a_m), @tag(address-key)
$$

and its sampler turns $\key_a$ into the uniform variates it consumes. Because $\key_a$ depends
on $\key$ and $a$ alone, a quantity's draw does not depend on the order in which the model was
built, on what was retired before it, or on which branch holds it, and a namespace's keys share the
prefix of folds that the namespace's own address determines.

The independence of draws rests on an idealization: that $\operatorname{fold}$ and
$\operatorname{split}$ behave as a pseudorandom function, so that distinct addresses yield keys
indistinguishable from independent uniform ones. Two things the core must ensure for that to mean
anything. Distinct addresses must give distinct inputs: each segment's encoding must be injective
and tagged by its kind, so that the name `"3"` and the integer $3$ differ, and folding segment by
segment, rather than hashing a concatenated string, rules out collisions such as `ab/c` against
`a/bc`. And each batch row of a run must have its own run key, split from the run's, so that rows
are independent.

Because the uniform variates are drawn on the host from the key and then converted to the
precision a query computes in, one key gives the same draws at single and double precision, up to
the rounding of the conversion.

## Retirement

Retiring is the one change that shrinks a model: the quantities under a namespace are removed, and
what they said about the rest is kept. Retirement is read on the probabilistic graph. Let $E$ be
the quantities to retire, $\mathcal T_E$ the terms joined to any of them ($V_t \cap E \neq \emptyset$),
and

$$
	B = \Bigl(\bigcup_{t \in \mathcal T_E} V_t\Bigr) \setminus E @tag(blanket)
$$

their *Markov blanket*: the other quantities joined to those terms. In the example, retiring the
first tick, $E = \{\randomPoint_0, \bfz_0\}$, touches the terms $t_1$, $t_3$, $t_4$ and $t_6$,
and its blanket is $\{\randomPoint_1, \bfm, \bfzeta_1, \bfsigma\}$: both parameters join it,
because the motion model $t_3$ and the likelihood factor $t_6$ they condition are among the terms
replaced. The score $t_7$ of $\bfsigma$ is not touched, and stays.

Retirement must condition as well as marginalize. The retired namespace of a tick holds that tick's
measurements as well as its states, and integrating the measurements out of the joint would discard
exactly the information a sliding window keeps them for. So the quantities of $E$ split into those
fixed at given values, $E_O$ (usually the measurements, with the values the estimator observed),
and those integrated out, $E_F$. Since conditioning belongs to the query, the values of $E_O$ are
given to the retirement itself, and once retired they become part of the model: what remains is the
joint of the remaining quantities given those values.

<Statement {...retirement} />

<Proof>

Split [](eq:log-density) into the terms of $\mathcal T_E$, which involve only $E$ and $B$, and the
rest, which involve no quantity of $E$. Fixing $E_O$ and integrating over $E_F$, the second group
comes out of the integral as a constant factor, by Tonelli's theorem for the non-negative
integrand, and what remains of the first group is $\exp \logterm_B(\point_B)$.

</Proof>

The replacement must be computed from the terms $\mathcal T_E$ alone. Computing the marginal of the
whole model on $B$ instead would fold in the terms outside $\mathcal T_E$, which the model still
holds, and count them twice. The integral defining $\logterm_B$ rarely has a closed form. It has one
for linear-Gaussian terms, where retiring the oldest state of a chain is the update-and-predict step
of a Kalman filter; otherwise it is approximated, by a Laplace approximation at the current
estimate in the archive's sliding windows. Either way, computing it needs the distributions and the
linear algebra of later entries, which the core does not have.

**What the core does.** The core retires; it does not compute the replacement. A retirement is
given a namespace and the values of $E_O$, and goes in three steps.

1. The core reads $E$, the quantities under the namespace, and $E_F = E \setminus E_O$; it reads
   $\mathcal T_E$ off the probabilistic graph, and $B$ by [](eq:blanket). It hands these to the
   caller with the values of $E_O$: the terms with their log-densities in the compilation graph,
   and the blanket's quantities with their types.
2. The caller builds the replacement $\logterm_B$, a likelihood factor added to the compilation
   graph as any term's log-density is, from the quantities of $B$ and constants: exactly the
   integral of [%full](statement:retirement), or an approximation of it, as the caller chooses. When
   it is one density over the blanket's tangent coordinates jointly, its natural type is the product
   of the blanket's types. The caller may also give a proposal for each blanket quantity the
   retirement leaves unscored (below).
3. The core checks the replacement and applies the change. It refuses a replacement reached from a
   quantity outside $B$, read off the compilation graph as every term's $V_t$ is: one reached from a
   quantity of $E$ would be removed with it, and one reached from a quantity outside the blanket
   would add a dependence the retired terms did not have. Otherwise it removes $E$, the terms
   $\mathcal T_E$ and the mapped values reached from $E$, and adds $\logterm_B$, in one change: no
   reader of the model sees it partly retired.

The core cannot check that the replacement is the integral it stands for, or how good an
approximation of it is; that is the caller's to answer for. When it is exact, every query of the new
model has the density of the corresponding query of the old one, with $E_F$ integrated out and
$E_O$ fixed, by [%full](statement:retirement).

Two consequences for the rest of the model:

- **Mapped values.** Retirement removes the quantities $E$, the terms $\mathcal T_E$, and exactly
  the mapped values reached from $E$ in the compilation graph; every other node stays, whatever its
  address. The namespace selects $E$ and nothing else. Removal is safe because every node reached
  from $E$ is a mapped value reached from $E$ or the log-density of a term in $\mathcal T_E$: a
  term whose log-density is reached from a quantity of $E$ has that quantity among $V_t$. In the
  example, the step $r = \point_1 - \point_0$ of the compilation graph above is reached from
  $\randomPoint_0$, and goes with it, as does every value between it and $\logterm_6$. A mapped
  value reached from $E$ that a user holds as a target is removed with the rest, and is no longer
  available after the retirement.
- **Blanket quantities may become parameters.** A blanket quantity whose scoring term was in
  $\mathcal T_E$ ($\randomPoint_1$ in the example, whose motion model $t_3$ was conditioned on
  $\randomPoint_0$) is scored by no term afterwards, and so, by the definition in
  [The probabilistic graph](#the-probabilistic-graph), it is a parameter. That changes nothing a
  query computes. [%full](statement:retirement) holds at every value of it; the replacement is
  conditioned on it, as a score is conditioned on a parameter with an improper prior; and a query
  leaves it free or observes it as before. Its uncertainty is a matter of the query's target, not
  of the quantity's kind, and is what it was. In the measure theory of
  [parameters and improper priors](../../math/generative-models/#parameters-and-improper-priors),
  the new model is a family indexed by it, and $\exp \logterm_B$ is its score: for linear-Gaussian
  terms, the Kalman filter's predicted density of $\randomPoint_1$ times a constant, a proper prior
  written as a score. What changes is generation, as for every parameter: the quantity is drawn
  from a proposal when it has one, which the caller may give with the replacement, and generation
  is refused for it otherwise. With the Kalman prediction as its proposal, $\logterm_B$ and the
  proposal's log-density cancel in the weight [](eq:weight) up to a constant, so the draw is as
  good as an exact sampler's. The core never writes a replacement in generative form, as a term
  scoring a blanket quantity with a sampler: a proposal gives generation all it needs.

**Testing retirement.** The core's tests exercise retirement on a model whose replacement is known
in closed form. A private normal distribution with constant variance, written in the tests as a
composite like the sphere of [the acceptance test](#the-acceptance-test), builds a scalar chain

$$
	\randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(a, s^2), \quad
	\bfz_0 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, r^2), \quad
	\randomPoint_1 \mid \randomPoint_0 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_0, q^2), \quad
	\bfz_1 \mid \randomPoint_1 \sim \mathcal N(\randomPoint_1, r^2),
$$

with constants $a$, $s$, $r$ and $q$. Retiring $E = \{\randomPoint_0, \bfz_0\}$ with $\bfz_0$
observed at $\hat z_0$ touches the terms of $\randomPoint_0$, $\bfz_0$ and $\randomPoint_1$, and its
blanket is $\{\randomPoint_1\}$. Completing the square in $\point_0$ gives the update
$\mathcal N(a, s^2)(\point_0)\, \mathcal N(\point_0, r^2)(\hat z_0) = \mathcal N(a, s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0)\, \mathcal N(\mu, P)(\point_0)$,
and integrating the motion against it gives the prediction, so that

$$
	\logterm_B(\point_1) = \log \mathcal N(a, s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0) + \log \mathcal N(\mu, P + q^2)(\point_1),
	\qquad
	K = \frac{s^2}{s^2 + r^2}, \quad \mu = a + K(\hat z_0 - a), \quad P = (1 - K)\, s^2, @tag(kalman)
$$

with $\mathcal N(m, v)(\point)$ the normal density of mean $m$ and variance $v$ at $\point$. The
tests check:

1. that $E_F$, $\mathcal T_E$ and $B$ are as above, and that exactly the nodes the rule for mapped
   values names are removed;
2. that a replacement reached from $\randomPoint_0$, or from a quantity outside the blanket, is
   refused, and leaves the model as it was;
3. that with [](eq:kalman) as the replacement, the new model's log-density and the derivatives of a
   query free in $\randomPoint_1$ agree, constants included, with the old model's with
   $\point_0$ integrated out by quadrature and $\bfz_0$ fixed at $\hat z_0$, as
   [%full](statement:retirement) says they must;
4. that generation is refused for $\randomPoint_1$ without a proposal, and that with the proposal
   $\mathcal N(\mu, P + q^2)$ every generated point has the same weight, the evidence
   $\log \mathcal N(a, s^2 + r^2)(\hat z_0)$.

## In skoro's terms

- **Quantities** are Euclidean (real, vector, matrix), positive ($\positive^n$, with the
  retraction [](eq:positive)), and discrete (categorical, boolean, integer), and products of
  continuous quantities. Later entries add curved types as embedded types, each declared by its
  retraction and that retraction's inverse, written as operations on storage, in whatever form the
  type's geometry suggests and smooth as computed at the identity of its charts. The core provides
  their boundary operations, the inclusion and the retraction, whose derivatives follow by
  [%full](statement:embedded) from passes over those two operations. Dimensions are fixed when a quantity is created, and every
  derivative coordinate is indexed by tangent dimension.
- **The compilation graph** holds values and operations, and programs are compiled from it; **the
  probabilistic graph** holds quantities and terms, and is read off the compilation graph and each
  term's scored quantity.
- **Operations** are maps [](eq:operation) with up to four derivative maps over frames, each with
  an order. The core's primitives are those of [The primitives](#the-primitives) and the boundary
  operations of embedded types; every other operation, the product's retraction included, is a
  composite of them or a fused operation, which a feature crate may write over the core's tensor
  layer, tested against the composite it fuses.
- **Folding** replaces a value reached only from fixed inputs with a root: in the model when they
  are constants, in a compiled query when they include observed quantities.
- **Passes** are the forward, tangent, cotangent and Hessian passes over a compiled program,
  differentiating with respect to a query's free continuous quantities in their charts, refused
  before running when not admissible by [%full](statement:order).
- **The density** is the sum of terms [](eq:log-density). A query fixes the observed quantities,
  continuous and discrete, and its density [](eq:query) is a function of the free ones,
  differentiated in the continuous ones alone. Generation is ancestral, from the samplers of
  scored quantities and the proposals the core supplies for parameters, and its weight is
  [](eq:weight).
- **Random keys** are derived from addresses by [](eq:address-key).
- **Retirement** removes a namespace's quantities $E$, conditioning on the given values of $E_O$ and
  integrating out $E_F$, and replaces the terms $\mathcal T_E$ with one likelihood factor over the
  blanket [](eq:blanket). The core computes $E$, $\mathcal T_E$ and $B$, checks the replacement and
  applies the change; the caller computes the replacement. A blanket quantity left unscored becomes a
  parameter, generated from a proposal the caller may give.

## Appendix: the probability space of a model

A skoro model is a *model built forward* in the sense of
[models built forward, for the measure theorist](../../math/generative-models/), and every object
in this document has a place in that note's measure theory.

- **Quantities are the coordinates.** Each quantity lives in a space with a reference measure:
  Lebesgue measure on $\R^n$ and $\R^{m \times n}$ and on the open subset $\positive^n$, counting
  measure on $\mathrm{Categorical}(n)$ and on $\mathbb Z$, and product measures on products. The
  probability space can be taken to be the product of the quantities' spaces, with the quantities as
  its coordinates, and the probabilistic graph's ancestral order as the order in which they were
  built.
- **Terms are conditional densities.** A term that scores a quantity has, as its $\exp \logterm_t$,
  that quantity's conditional density given those it is conditioned on, so [](eq:log-density) is the
  logarithm of the joint density of
  [%full](statement:math/generative-models/joint-law). A likelihood factor is the change of measure
  of [%full](statement:math/generative-models/likelihood-factor).
- **Mapped values are functions of the quantities,** and by
  [%full](statement:math/generative-models/doob-dynkin) they are all the random variables a model can
  express. Each is determined by the quantities it is computed from, so it is generated with them
  and weighed through them, and needs no term of its own.
- **A query conditions by dividing densities.** Its log-density [](eq:query) is that of the
  regular conditional distribution of [%full](statement:math/generative-models/conditioning), up to
  an additive constant, which is why a query may drop it.
- **Every number an algorithm estimates is an integral** against the joint density, by
  [%full](statement:math/generative-models/integral): an expectation, a marginal, the evidence. Generation draws from the conditional densities in the order the model was built,
  which is Ionescu-Tulcea's construction, and importance weighting [](eq:weight) estimates such an
  integral from those draws.
- **Retirement integrates and conditions.** [%full](statement:retirement) integrates the retired
  free quantities out of the terms that touch them and conditions on the retired observed ones,
  which is the same marginalization and the same division of densities, carried out once and kept.

An embedded type, such as the acceptance test's sphere, has no reference measure in this sense,
since it is not an open subset of a coordinate space; the sphere carries no density in the core's
tests. Manifold-valued quantities beyond the
positive orthant will need reference measures of their own (surface measure on a sphere, Haar
measure on the rotations) when they arrive.

## Open questions

None remain.

## Revisions

**2026-10-14.** Reopened because an embedded type's retraction was fixed as
$P(\point + B(\point)\, \tangent)$, so a type could not declare a retraction of another form,
such as $R \operatorname{Exp}(\tangent)$ on the rotations. An embedded type is now declared by its
retraction $R$ and its inverse $Q$ alone, written as operations on storage in any form, and the
core's boundary operations are the inclusion and the retraction itself, whose derivative maps
[%full](statement:embedded) assembles from passes over $R$ and $Q$. The tangent basis is now the
derivative of the retraction, and need no longer be orthonormal, since the inverse retraction
supplies the derivative of the chart at the output. The acceptance test's sphere now uses a
retraction that is not a projection. The elementwise sine, cosine and two-argument inverse tangent
were added to the primitives, so that retractions built from them can be composites. An optional
projection and optional closed forms of $B$ and $L$ were considered during the reopening and left
out: the projection has no use in the core and can be added later without changing a declared type,
and the closed forms are derivatives written by hand, a second path to every boundary factor, whose
only benefit is speed no measurement has yet asked for. What they would have avoided, the
removable singularity of an exponential map's formula at $\tangent = 0$, is ruled out by a
requirement instead: a type's $R$ and $Q$ must be smooth as computed there.
